;;; A fact written with a `:rule` leaves every core: the refutation that rests on it reports the
;;; empty core —
;;; M1f **W87**, register row `W87/written-rule-leaves-core`, filed by S1f.7.9 (T1f.7.9.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One guest, the fact `(paid Ann :rule zzz)`, a rule `seat` that seats whoever has paid and a
;;; rule `clash` that forbids being seated and having paid. Root is dead. No candidate. The
;;; twin, `written-rule-leaves-core-twin.ein`, writes `(paid Ann)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The refutation rests on the one fact the program writes, `(paid Ann)`: the twin's core.
;;; Under F24's (c), and under (b) with a declared rule, a written fact with a provenance is a
;;; given in every core, so the core is `(paid Ann)`. Under (a), and under (b) for `zzz`, the
;;; program is refused at load.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `91b12be8`, release: R is empty, root dead, `unsat core ∅, the empty set: the refutation
;;; rests on no fact`, `"core": []` in the summary and in `nogoods.json`, `refuted by clash`.
;;; The loader stores a written `:rule` fact as a derivation with no premises, so the core
;;; search follows it to nothing. `:rule seat`, a declared rule, gives the same.
;;; Open, and F24's: what a written provenance may claim is the user's question, not ruled.

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(relation seated Guest :why "{?1} is seated")
(is-a Ann Guest)
(paid Ann :rule zzz)
(rule seat () :match (paid ?g) :assert (seated ?g) :why "{?g} is seated: they have paid")
(rule clash () :match (and (seated ?g) (paid ?g)) :assert (false) :why "{?g} is seated and has paid, which this party forbids")
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
